    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "PAINTED BY J. P. BUTTERWORTH\nI. Donald Mo Kay Oog. Builder of the Leviathon\nCLIPPER SHIP \"GREAT REPUBLIC\"\nLITH. BY N. CURRIER",
      "ThE PRESTDENT\nSherry Dubonnet Campari Try Vamoute Sake\nManhattan Martini\nBloody Mary Soretiver Whiskey Sour\nTom Collins Volka Collins\nScotch Bourbon Canadian\nGin Vodka\nOUR SELECTiON OF VINTAGE WiNES\nGreat Wines of Burgundy Great Wines of Bordeaux\nChampagne\nCointreau Drambuie Bénetticine\nCièmo do Mentho Cognac VS P Bénitine and Brant\nSPECAL\nSOUP\nDouble Consemene with Sherry\n*am a\nENTREES\nBroiled Filet Mignon - Maitre d'Hotel\nx=xx8xx-z2\nVichy Carrots\nCurrial Lumb Matras with Rice Pitaf\nDESSERT\nFruit Tartlet\n4:3 01\nAmerican Coffee\nOrange Pekoe Tea\nJapanese\nGreen Tea\nHAN*\nHAM*\nPAN AMERiCAN CUiSiNE BY MAXiM'S DE PARiS",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"GREAT REPUBLIC\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Mariners Museum, Newport News, Virginia.\nThe clipper ship \"Great Republic\" stood for the summit of achievement in the world of sailing\nships. She was, from the standpoint of size, appearance and cost, exactly what her builder Donald\nMcKay had originally intended to name her: The King of Clippers. The triumph of marine archi-\ntecture was launched in 1853 in East Boston. The \"Great Republic\" was considered the largest,\nsharpest and most magnificent craft that was ever produced by any nation of age.\nOn the night of December 26th, before she had barely tasted salt water, fire broke out on\nFront Street in New York and within minutes, the rigging of the \"Great Republic\", lying at wharf,\nwas a network of flames. The ship was rebuilt, but her former tonnage of 4,555 tons was reduced\nand her sail plan and spars cut down so that she needed only half of her original 130 man crew.\nNevertheless, she served her new owners well and remained the largest merchant ship of her time.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world... to Japan... to Australia... and, in the days of the\ngreat gold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California.\nOur current equipment...the the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century... consists of the finest\ncommercial aircraft ever developed—aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record\nfor safety, speed and service.\n*CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG US PAT OFFICE-COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS INC\nLITHO IN USA -8739-0393\nW13.096.028"
    ],
